          lemma: "Σὺ εἶ ὁ ἐρχόμενος;",
          title: "The certifier doubts",
          body: "Remember who is asking. John is the man who pointed Jesus out at the Jordan, who protested *I need to be baptized by you*, who staked his whole prophetic career on the identification. Now he sits in Herod's fortress at Machaerus, arrested for telling the truth, and the reports reaching his cell do not match the job description he himself had announced: he had promised an axe at the root, a winnowing fork, fire — and the news is of healed lepers and dinner parties. So the greatest man born of women (v. 11 — Jesus' own words) sends the question every honest believer eventually asks in some form: **have I got this wrong?** Notice what the gospel does *not* do with the question. It does not suppress it (this scene survives in two gospels); it does not scold it; and Jesus' verdict on the asker, delivered the moment the messengers turn away, is not disappointment but the highest praise he ever gives anyone. Doubt, in this text, is not the opposite of faith; it is something faith passes through when the evidence arrives in a different shape than the promise seemed to have. If this project has a patron saint, he is sitting in that cell.",
