      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a11.6.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g11.5.16', 'g11.6.7'],
          lemma: "πτωχοὶ εὐαγγελίζονται … μὴ σκανδαλισθῇ",
          title: "The answer that leaves the prison door shut",
          body: "Jesus does not answer *yes*. He answers with a list — and the list is Scripture: blind seeing, lame walking, deaf hearing, dead raised, poor hearing good news is Isaiah's portrait of the age when God finally comes (Isa 35:5-6; 26:19; 61:1). John would have caught every allusion. But he would also have caught the **omission**, because Isaiah 61 continues: *…to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to those who are bound* — the one clause Jesus leaves out, speaking to messengers who will walk back to a prison. That is what the closing beatitude is for, and it is addressed to John personally: **blessed is whoever is not scandalized by me** — σκανδαλισθῇ, tripped by the stumbling-stone of a God who is doing wonders *and not doing the one you need*. The answer, decoded: *the age has truly begun; the evidence is real; and your door stays shut, and I will not pretend otherwise; blessed are you if you can bear that*. John was executed in that prison. Anyone who has prayed beside a hospital bed knows exactly which clause of Isaiah went unfulfilled for them, and this passage is the gospel's most honest acknowledgement that the kingdom's *already* and its *not yet* can run straight through one person's cell.",
        },
      ],
