      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a11.21.1',
          kind: "text",
          refs: ['g11.21.3', 'g11.23.14'],
          lemma: "Χοραζείν … Σοδόμοις",
          title: "Chorazin — woes over towns we never saw",
          body: "**Chorazin** appears nowhere else in the gospel record: no miracle at Chorazin is narrated in any gospel, yet this sentence assumes the town saw *most of his mighty works*. That casual gap is worth pausing on — the gospels themselves testify that they are anthologies, not archives (John says so outright: *many other things Jesus did… not written*). A fabricator invents stories to match his punchlines; Matthew preserves a punchline whose stories he doesn't have. The woe itself works by a scandalous comparison: **Tyre and Sidon** were the prophets' stock pagan cities, and **Sodom** the Bible's byword for irrecoverable wickedness — and Jesus ranks them all *above* the devout lakeside towns that heard him preach and stayed unmoved. The principle underneath is stated as courtroom logic: judgement is indexed to light received, not to sins committed. Capernaum's taunt — *exalted to heaven? down to Hades!* — is recycled from Isaiah's mockery of Babylon (Isa 14:13-15), the empire's epitaph pinned on a fishing village that merely *hosted the kingdom politely and did nothing*. Note where the chapter goes next: straight from these woes into *I thank you, Father* — as if the failure of the strategy were somehow inside the plan.",
        },
      ],
