          lemma: "Δεῦτε πρός με … ὁ ζυγός μου χρηστὸς",
          title: "The invitation at the bottom of the chapter",
          body: "Follow the chapter's descent to see where this lands. John doubts; the towns shrug; the generation heckles both messengers; the woes fall — and then, out of that wreckage, the gentlest sentence in the gospels: **come to me, all you who labour and are heavy-laden**. The addressees are not the successful; κοπιῶντες are the worn-out, πεφορτισμένοι the *loaded-down* — in context, above all those buckling under religion itself as the scribes had built it (23:4 uses the same word for the *heavy burdens* the experts tie onto shoulders). The offer works by a paradox of yokes. The rabbis spoke beautifully of taking on *the yoke of Torah*, *the yoke of the kingdom of heaven*; Jesus does not offer yokelessness — a yoke is still a working harness, and his school still has a curriculum (*learn from me*) — he offers **a different yoke**, and describes the teacher rather than the syllabus: πραΰς, gentle, and ταπεινὸς τῇ καρδίᾳ, *lowly in heart* — the only place in all the gospels where Jesus describes his own heart. The promised **rest for your souls** is lifted from Jeremiah 6:16, where God offers it at the crossroads *and the people answer: we will not walk in it*. This chapter has just re-run Jeremiah's scene — the offer, the refusal — and re-issues the offer anyway. And χρηστός does not mean ”easy”: it means *kind* — the word for well-fitted, non-chafing tack. Not: nothing will be asked. Rather: what is asked will be shaped to the one who carries it, by a carpenter who, tradition says, made yokes.",
